      "created_at": "2026-06-12T12:27:59Z",
      "updated_at": "2026-06-12T12:32:51Z",
      "body": "> My use case is that I have two storage plugins that both subscribe to the same key expression - but one is complete and the other is an incomplete fallback (more specifically, one is a local rocksdb and the other is an eventually consistent S3 replica). It sounds like what you're saying is that the second storage plugin declaration (complete = false) overrides the first storage plugin declaration (complete = true) and so there isn't a way to use `target` to filter?\n\nPlugins don't share the same session, so my reasoning above doesn't apply. To simulate a multi-session scenario you can write:\n\n```nushell\n#!/usr/bin/env nuze -X0\n\nzenoh runtime open r {mode:router}\nzenoh session open --runtime r -s scomplete\nzenoh session open --runtime r -s sincomplete\n\nlet qcomplete = job spawn {\n  zenoh queryable -s scomplete k --complete true { |s| \"complete\" }\n}\n\nsleep 500ms\n\nlet qincomplete = job spawn {\n  zenoh queryable -s sincomplete k --complete false { |s| \"incomplete\" }\n}\n```\n\n(run the above with `nuze -X0e 'source <file.nu>'`)\n\n```console\n$ nuze -X0e 'zenoh open {mode:client}'\n> zenoh get k --target all --consolidation none | get payload\n\u256d\u2500\u2500\u2500\u252c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u256e\n\u2502 0 \u2502 incomplete \u2502\n\u2502 1 \u2502 complete   \u2502\n\u2570\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2534\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u256f\n> zenoh get k --target all-complete --consolidation none | get payload\n\u256d\u2500\u2500\u2500\u252c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u256e\n\u2502 0 \u2502 complete \u2502\n\u2570\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2534\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u256f\n```\n\nThus I cannot reproduce the issue. If I were to use one session only:\n\n```nushell\n#!/usr/bin/env nuze -X0\n\nzenoh open {mode:\"router\"}\n\nlet qcomplete = job spawn {\n  zenoh queryable k --complete true { |s| \"complete\" }\n}\n\nsleep 500ms\n\nlet qincomplete = job spawn {\n  zenoh queryable k --complete false { |s| \"incomplete\" }\n}\n```\n\n```console\n$ nuze -X0e 'zenoh open {mode:client}'\n> zenoh get k --target all --consolidation none | get payload\n\u256d\u2500\u2500\u2500\u252c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u256e\n\u2502 0 \u2502 incomplete \u2502\n\u2502 1 \u2502 complete   \u2502\n\u2570\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2534\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u256f\n> zenoh get k --target all-complete --consolidation none | get payload\n\u256d\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u256e\n\u2502 empty list \u2502\n\u2570\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u256f\n```\n\nNow this corresponds to what you observe, yet it doesn't line up with the scenario you describe.\n\nTo conclude, I believe the \"same-session completeness overriding\" behavior is not the root cause of your issue, something else is going on.",
